    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d494">

                <pb facs="0862" n="730" xml:id="pg730"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860c.01/6–3045: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 494<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State ad interim</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-30T14:00:00+03:00">June 30, 1945—2
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2345. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polco</hi>. Sent Dept as 2345 rptd London as
                    332; ReEmb’s 2334, June 29, midnight.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d494-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <p>… Bierut in his conversation with me as reported in my 2259 June 25, 5
                        p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d494-f2"/> did not raise the constitutional
                    question in connection with the legality of the obligations of the pre-war Govt.
                    He did indicate that he wished to scrutinize the transactions of the Govt in
                    Exile in order to satisfy himself that they were undertaken in the interests of
                    the Polish State. This seemed to me a reasonable attitude because of the
                    controversies that have existed between the two groups. Bierut and all the Poles
                    show the keenest desire to obtain at the earliest moment the assets of the
                    Polish State abroad and economic assistance from the US in their vast task of
                    reconstruction. …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
